1
00:00:00,112 --> 00:00:01,725
It's 6:40 in the morning,

2
00:00:01,888 --> 00:00:03,675
Sarah is on the train to work,

3
00:00:03,850 --> 00:00:06,212
and she's already running late on her own life.

4
00:00:06,425 --> 00:00:08,200
She opens the list on her phone:

5
00:00:08,375 --> 00:00:11,962
twenty-three things. Answer every email before nine,

6
00:00:12,088 --> 00:00:14,150
go to the gym four times a week,

7
00:00:14,188 --> 00:00:15,138
call her mom,

8
00:00:15,312 --> 00:00:16,825
finish the quarterly report,

9
00:00:16,938 --> 00:00:18,400
learn Python. And,

10
00:00:18,562 --> 00:00:19,375
near the bottom,

11
00:00:19,538 --> 00:00:20,700
be more relaxed.

12
00:00:20,912 --> 00:00:22,237
And here's the strange part:

13
00:00:22,450 --> 00:00:24,925
Sarah hasn't done anything wrong yet today.

14
00:00:25,150 --> 00:00:26,838
She hasn't even had breakfast,

15
00:00:26,862 --> 00:00:29,575
and still, she already feels like she's failing.

16
00:00:29,788 --> 00:00:32,100
If you've ever finished a busy day and still

17
00:00:32,100 --> 00:00:33,400
felt you hadn't done enough,

18
00:00:33,500 --> 00:00:34,788
this video is for you.

19
00:00:35,000 --> 00:00:37,625
Because that feeling comes from a very old voice,

20
00:00:37,725 --> 00:00:39,688
and it speaks in one tiny word.

21
00:00:40,314 --> 00:00:41,514
To understand that word,

22
00:00:41,602 --> 00:00:43,302
you need to know Sarah a little better.

23
00:00:43,526 --> 00:00:45,064
She's a senior analyst,

24
00:00:45,102 --> 00:00:46,802
she's brilliant with numbers,

25
00:00:46,814 --> 00:00:49,276
and everyone at the office trusts her work more

26
00:00:49,276 --> 00:00:50,014
than she does.

27
00:00:50,226 --> 00:00:52,664
In the elevator she reads her list again,

28
00:00:52,852 --> 00:00:55,914
and notices that almost every line starts the same

29
00:00:55,914 --> 00:00:57,714
way. I should answer faster.

30
00:00:57,926 --> 00:00:59,927
I should be further in my career by now.

31
00:01:00,139 --> 00:01:02,239
I should be happy for my friend's promotion,

32
00:01:02,426 --> 00:01:03,639
even though it stings.

33
00:01:03,852 --> 00:01:04,789
In other words,

34
00:01:04,839 --> 00:01:06,689
it isn't really a list of plans.

35
00:01:06,902 --> 00:01:08,176
It's a list of rules,

36
00:01:08,176 --> 00:01:11,052
and every rule carries a small sentence attached:

37
00:01:11,264 --> 00:01:12,102
and if you don't,

38
00:01:12,314 --> 00:01:13,476
you're falling behind.

39
00:01:13,689 --> 00:01:15,152
And in twenty-three lines,

40
00:01:15,164 --> 00:01:17,976
there isn't a single thing Sarah actually wants.

41
00:01:18,608 --> 00:01:20,658
On Saturday, her one free day,

42
00:01:20,833 --> 00:01:22,320
Sarah decides to rest.

43
00:01:22,545 --> 00:01:24,920
She makes a coffee and puts on a movie,

44
00:01:25,083 --> 00:01:27,896
the Saturday she's been promising herself for weeks.

45
00:01:28,133 --> 00:01:30,533
But twenty minutes later a thought sits down next

46
00:01:30,533 --> 00:01:32,996
to her: she should be using this time better.

47
00:01:33,220 --> 00:01:34,633
The kitchen is a mess,

48
00:01:34,670 --> 00:01:35,920
the report is waiting,

49
00:01:36,095 --> 00:01:37,858
and she still hasn't called her mom.

50
00:01:38,070 --> 00:01:39,020
And just like that,

51
00:01:39,183 --> 00:01:42,033
the movie stops being a reward and starts feeling

52
00:01:42,033 --> 00:01:42,658
like a crime.

53
00:01:42,883 --> 00:01:44,283
So she pauses the movie,

54
00:01:44,370 --> 00:01:45,408
just for a second,

55
00:01:45,595 --> 00:01:48,008
and at eleven at night she's still scrubbing the

56
00:01:48,008 --> 00:01:49,670
kitchen. She didn't rest,

57
00:01:49,720 --> 00:01:51,258
she didn't enjoy the cleaning,

58
00:01:51,358 --> 00:01:52,808
and she never called her mom.

59
00:01:53,020 --> 00:01:55,746
The day simply vanished between what she should do

60
00:01:55,746 --> 00:01:57,258
and the guilt of not doing it.

61
00:01:57,898 --> 00:02:00,674
The next evening is her best friend's birthday dinner.

62
00:02:00,898 --> 00:02:02,699
Sarah gives a lovely toast,

63
00:02:02,748 --> 00:02:05,086
then checks her work email under the table,

64
00:02:05,274 --> 00:02:07,874
and before dessert she leaves early to finish the

65
00:02:07,874 --> 00:02:09,236
report. And then,

66
00:02:09,336 --> 00:02:10,324
on the way home,

67
00:02:10,511 --> 00:02:12,198
she feels guilty for leaving,

68
00:02:12,286 --> 00:02:13,548
because a good friend,

69
00:02:13,711 --> 00:02:15,686
she thinks, should have stayed.

70
00:02:15,911 --> 00:02:17,011
But if she had stayed,

71
00:02:17,186 --> 00:02:19,198
she'd have felt guilty about the report.

72
00:02:19,424 --> 00:02:20,348
That's the trap:

73
00:02:20,448 --> 00:02:21,961
when everything is a should,

74
00:02:22,086 --> 00:02:23,811
there's no right choice left,

75
00:02:24,024 --> 00:02:26,074
only different ways of feeling guilty.

76
00:02:26,286 --> 00:02:27,586
Whatever Sarah picks,

77
00:02:27,611 --> 00:02:29,023
some rule gets broken,

78
00:02:29,186 --> 00:02:30,873
and she's the one who pays for it.

79
00:02:31,513 --> 00:02:33,075
So let's put a face to that word.

80
00:02:33,288 --> 00:02:34,288
Meet The Should,

81
00:02:34,450 --> 00:02:37,400
the voice that turns every wish into a duty

82
00:02:37,400 --> 00:02:39,388
and every rest into a debt.

83
00:02:39,600 --> 00:02:40,575
It doesn't shout.

84
00:02:40,788 --> 00:02:42,050
It sounds reasonable,

85
00:02:42,250 --> 00:02:45,500
almost wise, like a strict teacher who only wants

86
00:02:45,500 --> 00:02:46,288
the best for you.

87
00:02:46,550 --> 00:02:47,538
"You should have done more.

88
00:02:47,750 --> 00:02:48,912
Other people manage it,

89
00:02:49,000 --> 00:02:51,588
so why can't you?" And because it sounds so

90
00:02:51,588 --> 00:02:53,600
reasonable, we rarely question it.

91
00:02:53,812 --> 00:02:55,150
We just assume it's right,

92
00:02:55,250 --> 00:02:56,325
and that we're the problem.

93
00:02:56,538 --> 00:02:57,800
And the more we obey it,

94
00:02:57,875 --> 00:03:00,212
the louder and more demanding it gets.

95
00:03:00,855 --> 00:03:03,380
The Should especially loves responsible people,

96
00:03:03,542 --> 00:03:05,580
the ones who grew up being the good kid,

97
00:03:05,767 --> 00:03:07,817
the one who never caused problems.

98
00:03:08,042 --> 00:03:09,392
So how can you tell it's with you?

99
00:03:09,642 --> 00:03:10,980
Four signs show up a lot.

100
00:03:11,192 --> 00:03:13,042
Your to-do list never ends,

101
00:03:13,105 --> 00:03:15,992
because every item you cross off brings two more.

102
00:03:16,205 --> 00:03:17,642
Resting feels like cheating.

103
00:03:17,867 --> 00:03:19,630
You say I should far more often than I

104
00:03:19,630 --> 00:03:22,367
want. And you're harder on yourself than you'd ever

105
00:03:22,367 --> 00:03:23,204
be on a friend.

106
00:03:23,417 --> 00:03:25,342
If you recognized two or more,

107
00:03:25,380 --> 00:03:27,930
don't worry. It doesn't mean something is wrong with

108
00:03:27,930 --> 00:03:30,192
you. It just means The Should has been working

109
00:03:30,192 --> 00:03:32,534
overtime. And here's the twist:

110
00:03:32,734 --> 00:03:34,796
all those shoulds don't make Sarah do more.

111
00:03:35,022 --> 00:03:36,184
They make her do less.

112
00:03:36,397 --> 00:03:37,434
On Monday morning,

113
00:03:37,584 --> 00:03:40,059
the report she should have finished is still open

114
00:03:40,059 --> 00:03:40,746
on her screen.

115
00:03:40,959 --> 00:03:42,522
It's been there for two weeks,

116
00:03:42,522 --> 00:03:44,046
and every time she looks at it,

117
00:03:44,247 --> 00:03:46,396
it feels less like work and more like a

118
00:03:46,396 --> 00:03:49,196
punishment, so she checks one more email instead.

119
00:03:49,422 --> 00:03:52,384
That's a pattern psychologists see again and again:

120
00:03:52,559 --> 00:03:54,884
when we do something because we feel we have

121
00:03:54,884 --> 00:03:57,496
to, we tire out and give up sooner than

122
00:03:57,496 --> 00:03:59,109
when we do it because we want to.

123
00:03:59,334 --> 00:04:01,272
By turning the report into a duty,

124
00:04:01,384 --> 00:04:04,209
The Should drains the very energy Sarah needs to

125
00:04:04,209 --> 00:04:07,192
finish it. And The Should doesn't stay inside your

126
00:04:07,192 --> 00:04:08,692
head. That afternoon,

127
00:04:08,880 --> 00:04:11,380
Daniel brings Sarah a draft one day late,

128
00:04:11,555 --> 00:04:13,192
and before she can stop herself,

129
00:04:13,355 --> 00:04:14,155
she says it:

130
00:04:14,543 --> 00:04:17,818
"You should have sent this yesterday." Daniel's face drops,

131
00:04:17,880 --> 00:04:19,642
and Sarah regrets it instantly.

132
00:04:19,855 --> 00:04:22,330
It's the same tone she uses on herself every

133
00:04:22,330 --> 00:04:24,230
morning, and hearing it out loud,

134
00:04:24,468 --> 00:04:25,630
aimed at someone else,

135
00:04:25,818 --> 00:04:28,055
she realizes how harsh it really is.

136
00:04:28,280 --> 00:04:30,305
That's the thing about the shoulds we use on

137
00:04:30,305 --> 00:04:31,968
ourselves: little by little,

138
00:04:32,130 --> 00:04:34,343
the rules we live by become the rules we

139
00:04:34,343 --> 00:04:35,855
judge everyone else by.

140
00:04:36,489 --> 00:04:38,088
But The Should is not your enemy.

141
00:04:38,351 --> 00:04:39,538
For our ancestors,

142
00:04:39,601 --> 00:04:41,088
it worked more like a guardian.

143
00:04:41,301 --> 00:04:43,101
In a small group on the savanna,

144
00:04:43,301 --> 00:04:44,851
nobody survived alone,

145
00:04:45,014 --> 00:04:46,788
and every group had its rules:

146
00:04:46,864 --> 00:04:47,876
share your food,

147
00:04:48,064 --> 00:04:48,888
do your part,

148
00:04:48,976 --> 00:04:50,326
don't let the others down.

149
00:04:50,539 --> 00:04:52,751
And breaking those rules was dangerous,

150
00:04:52,751 --> 00:04:55,689
because whoever lost the group's trust could lose its

151
00:04:55,689 --> 00:04:58,114
protection too, and alone on the savanna,

152
00:04:58,301 --> 00:04:59,751
that could cost you your life.

153
00:04:59,964 --> 00:05:01,838
So guilt evolved as an alarm.

154
00:05:02,051 --> 00:05:04,276
It goes off when you break a rule and

155
00:05:04,276 --> 00:05:06,214
pushes you to repair things fast,

156
00:05:06,276 --> 00:05:09,126
to apologize, pay back and try harder.

157
00:05:09,351 --> 00:05:11,801
The ones who felt that alarm kept their place

158
00:05:11,801 --> 00:05:12,338
in the group,

159
00:05:12,451 --> 00:05:13,226
and with it,

160
00:05:13,314 --> 00:05:14,801
their chances of survival.

161
00:05:15,014 --> 00:05:17,638
And children who learned the group's rules quickly were

162
00:05:17,638 --> 00:05:21,138
safer, so our brains became experts at absorbing rules

163
00:05:21,138 --> 00:05:23,276
long before we're old enough to question them.

164
00:05:23,903 --> 00:05:26,965
Today, though, the rules come from everywhere:

165
00:05:27,178 --> 00:05:29,290
your parents, your first boss,

166
00:05:29,290 --> 00:05:31,678
and hundreds of strangers on your phone who seem

167
00:05:31,678 --> 00:05:34,865
to run ten kilometers before breakfast and never miss

168
00:05:34,865 --> 00:05:37,778
a deadline. The guardian can't tell a real rule

169
00:05:37,778 --> 00:05:38,802
from an imported one,

170
00:05:38,990 --> 00:05:40,952
so it treats all of them as if your

171
00:05:40,952 --> 00:05:42,640
place in the group depended on it.

172
00:05:42,865 --> 00:05:45,528
That's why a single unanswered email can feel like

173
00:05:45,528 --> 00:05:46,840
a small moral failure.

174
00:05:47,065 --> 00:05:48,615
The alarm isn't broken;

175
00:05:48,790 --> 00:05:50,890
it's just trying to protect you from a thousand

176
00:05:50,890 --> 00:05:52,740
rules that nobody really agreed on.

177
00:05:53,352 --> 00:05:54,352
A few days later,

178
00:05:54,514 --> 00:05:57,227
Elena finds Sarah eating lunch on the rooftop with

179
00:05:57,227 --> 00:05:58,177
her laptop open,

180
00:05:58,340 --> 00:06:00,564
sighing that she really should be over this by

181
00:06:00,564 --> 00:06:03,402
now. Elena smiles and asks her a question.

182
00:06:03,690 --> 00:06:05,027
"Every time you say should,

183
00:06:05,202 --> 00:06:08,014
whose voice is that?" Sarah starts to answer,

184
00:06:08,165 --> 00:06:11,164
then stops, because she has honestly never thought about

185
00:06:11,164 --> 00:06:12,977
it. Call your mom every day:

186
00:06:13,190 --> 00:06:14,027
that's her mom.

187
00:06:14,240 --> 00:06:16,414
Answer every email within the hour:

188
00:06:16,590 --> 00:06:17,627
her first boss,

189
00:06:17,827 --> 00:06:18,777
eight years ago.

190
00:06:19,002 --> 00:06:21,277
Be further in her career at thirty-four:

191
00:06:21,452 --> 00:06:23,590
the feed she scrolls every night.

192
00:06:23,802 --> 00:06:26,552
Then Elena asks the question that really matters:

193
00:06:26,814 --> 00:06:29,014
"And which of those rules do you actually agree

194
00:06:29,014 --> 00:06:31,040
with?" And that's the update.

195
00:06:31,252 --> 00:06:33,215
It isn't about dropping your standards,

196
00:06:33,290 --> 00:06:35,852
but about choosing which ones are really yours.

197
00:06:36,078 --> 00:06:37,427
Here are four ways to do it.

198
00:06:37,678 --> 00:06:38,590
First, catch it.

199
00:06:38,802 --> 00:06:41,115
Notice every time you think the word should,

200
00:06:41,215 --> 00:06:43,652
because you can't question a rule you don't even

201
00:06:43,652 --> 00:06:46,240
hear. Second, ask whose rule it is.

202
00:06:46,452 --> 00:06:48,552
If it's yours and you still believe in it,

203
00:06:48,565 --> 00:06:51,040
keep it. If it belongs to someone else,

204
00:06:51,065 --> 00:06:52,515
you're allowed to give it back.

205
00:06:52,778 --> 00:06:54,740
Third, swap should for could,

206
00:06:54,928 --> 00:06:55,665
or for want.

207
00:06:55,878 --> 00:06:57,628
I should go to the gym becomes I want

208
00:06:57,628 --> 00:06:59,878
to go, because I sleep better when I do.

209
00:07:00,090 --> 00:07:01,202
It sounds small,

210
00:07:01,252 --> 00:07:03,452
but it turns a duty into a choice,

211
00:07:03,465 --> 00:07:05,502
and choices are much easier to keep.

212
00:07:05,715 --> 00:07:08,152
And fourth, talk to yourself like you'd talk to

213
00:07:08,152 --> 00:07:10,140
a friend. It won't make you lazy:

214
00:07:10,252 --> 00:07:12,465
in a 2012 study at Berkeley,

215
00:07:12,540 --> 00:07:15,590
students who failed a hard test and were encouraged

216
00:07:15,590 --> 00:07:18,515
to treat themselves kindly then studied longer for the

217
00:07:18,515 --> 00:07:20,415
next one. So try it right now:

218
00:07:20,665 --> 00:07:22,740
think of one should that's been following you this

219
00:07:22,740 --> 00:07:24,352
week. Whose voice is it,

220
00:07:24,540 --> 00:07:26,002
and how would it sound as a could?

221
00:07:26,632 --> 00:07:27,482
A week later,

222
00:07:27,520 --> 00:07:28,657
on the evening train,

223
00:07:28,732 --> 00:07:30,695
Sarah's list has seven lines,

224
00:07:30,720 --> 00:07:32,482
and most of them start with want.

225
00:07:32,695 --> 00:07:33,970
Then she calls her mom,

226
00:07:34,145 --> 00:07:35,445
not because she should,

227
00:07:35,595 --> 00:07:36,882
but because she wants to.

228
00:07:37,095 --> 00:07:38,607
And for the first time in months,

229
00:07:38,645 --> 00:07:40,732
the call feels like a gift instead of a

230
00:07:40,732 --> 00:07:42,682
chore. The Should is still there,

231
00:07:42,720 --> 00:07:43,945
sitting on her shoulder,

232
00:07:44,032 --> 00:07:45,770
but smaller and quieter now,

233
00:07:45,970 --> 00:07:47,732
more like a reminder than a judge.

234
00:07:47,945 --> 00:07:49,095
So, a quick recap.

235
00:07:49,320 --> 00:07:52,082
The Should turns your wishes into duties and your

236
00:07:52,082 --> 00:07:53,008
rest into guilt.

237
00:07:53,220 --> 00:07:54,557
It exists because,

238
00:07:54,557 --> 00:07:55,807
for our ancestors,

239
00:07:55,832 --> 00:07:58,632
following the group's rules meant staying in the group.

240
00:07:58,857 --> 00:08:00,795
And you update it by catching the word,

241
00:08:00,958 --> 00:08:02,407
asking whose rule it is,

242
00:08:02,458 --> 00:08:03,845
swapping should for could,

243
00:08:04,045 --> 00:08:05,682
and being a friend to yourself.

244
00:08:05,907 --> 00:08:07,795
Because you are not a list of rules you

245
00:08:07,795 --> 00:08:09,795
keep failing. And you don't have to earn your

246
00:08:09,795 --> 00:08:11,732
rest. So tell me in the comments:

247
00:08:11,907 --> 00:08:13,745
what's one should you're ready to give back?

248
00:08:13,958 --> 00:08:16,945
And subscribe, because next time we're back with Daniel,

249
00:08:17,120 --> 00:08:19,620
and the voice that makes everything take longer than

250
00:08:19,620 --> 00:08:20,107
planned.
